{"version":1,"ops":[{"type":6,"author":{"id":"7faa25b6f26540223cd687923eda480087814e38"},"timestamp":1555163594,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDE1OlVzZXJDb250ZW50RWRpdDIwOTUwMzc4Mw=="},"target":"f87d74fb759fbc2d0227880c63be05a22b63b07e586aa8c95fb128188065d8a6","message":"Use case: HPC-related storage system with inode quotas and the need to stage data on it for HPC/HTC computation (which would talk to the staged data via `git-annex get` requests). For datasets with many small files this quickly becomes prohibitive, even when not using a full repo with an annex, but just a type-directory special remote annex object tree.\nIn a concrete case, any file \u003c200MB reduces the useable storage quota disproportionately.\n\nLimitations: HPC system often run a really old/stable software stack. In my case Git is version 1.8, for example. So the approach should ideally not use any advanced/non-standard technology. The recommendation by the operators is to prepare a SquashFS image for each computation and stage that. However, SquashFS images are read-only filesystems, so this would imply a one time export, and expensive operations after updating a single file. Dataset size typically varies from several GB up to ~1TB -- connectivity to such systems is usually good, but not *that* good.\n\nIdea: Use an uncompressed tarball on the receiving end as an archive using only a single inode. It seems `tar` is clever enough to not rewrite an archive when adding new content, or deleting references to obsolete content. Essentially, such a tarball would be an ever growing \"file system\" that contains an annex object tree, hence storage of multiple versions of a file is perfectly possible. The storage system in question is SSH-accessible, so a special-remote implementation would use SSH for transfer, and also to remote-execute `tar` command to test for, inject, or extract content.\n\n@datalad/developers @joeyh Is there already an implementation of such a special-remote that you are aware of? Do you see any show-stoppers?\n\nThx.","files":null},{"type":2,"author":{"id":"7faa25b6f26540223cd687923eda480087814e38"},"timestamp":1555163571,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDE3OlJlbmFtZWRUaXRsZUV2ZW50MjI3NDEyMzcyMw=="},"title":"tarball-base inode-minimizing special remote","was":"tarball-base inode-minimizing special remote"},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1555172949,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQ4MjgzMjcyNA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3327#issuecomment-482832724"},"message":"Pure tar would probably be really inefficient for addressing individual files later on. May be with some additional index to be able to position to a specific file it would work? If remote is Linux, may be could just be some fuse based overlay on top of something like that and then annex just using that location for regular special remote?","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1555173400,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQ4MjgzNDI5NA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3327#issuecomment-482834294"},"message":"All those are non-options. I will test read access latency with growing files again, didnt look to bad initially. Do you have suffered from that before?","files":null},{"type":6,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1555173400,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDE1OlVzZXJDb250ZW50RWRpdDIwOTUyNjkzNg=="},"target":"dc965bd25a1a8ba61ce80990a42e1cd250f1523bbbfba329e6e9af93b2031798","message":"All those are non-options. I will test read access latency with growing files again, didnt look too bad initially. Do you have suffered from that before?","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1555174584,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQ4MjgzODkzMA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3327#issuecomment-482838930"},"message":"Relevant BSC thesis:","files":null},{"type":6,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1555174584,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDE1OlVzZXJDb250ZW50RWRpdDIwOTUyOTk2OQ=="},"target":"50a8dade51faeb23db8b064b5e3ab05349ee719beaf878231f0acd8c75069d24","message":"Relevant BSC thesis: https://digitalcommons.wpi.edu/cgi/viewcontent.cgi?article=3116\u0026context=mqp-all","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1555175294,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQ4Mjg0MjA4MQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3327#issuecomment-482842081"},"message":"Suffered from what? If abuse if inodes by for annex - yes, and  I kept advocating to @joeyh finding ways to reduce it since day one of datalad. Recent work on v7 thin and may be some extra (to avoid annexed copy altogether) might eliminate it, at least for consuming use case. But here you seems to be looking for something beyond it - to reduce it for a  remote/backup, not at the point of use, right?","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1555179805,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQ4Mjg1ODI2Mw==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3327#issuecomment-482858263"},"message":"I am not even talking about the additional inodes used by git-annex. I am talking about having an git-annex accessible storage (container) that does not waste inodes on small files.\n\nStats example: A typical N=15, three-level GLM analysis of BOLD fMRI with FSL will come at about 35k files, but will only weigh 2.3GB in size (with standard resolution 3T BOLD data). When the storage provider calculates with ~200MB file size (as in my case), storing this result would cost the equivalent of 6.6GB (and this does not include any additional inode for the annex object tree, just actual files).","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1555180356,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQ4Mjg2MDE3MA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3327#issuecomment-482860170"},"message":"Alright, my re-test of the timing confirmed my initial assessment: \"not too bad\". I tar'ed /usr on my laptop (17GB), and extract files from the start and end of the archive. Examples:\n\n```\n% time tar -O -xf usr.tar usr/bin/zlib-flate \u003e /dev/null\ntar -O -xf usr.tar usr/bin/zlib-flate \u003e /dev/null  0.61s user 0.38s system 99% cpu 0.994 total\n\n% time tar -O -xf usr.tar usr/share/khelpcenter/plugins/Manpages/man1.desktop \u003e /dev/null\ntar -O -xf usr.tar usr/share/khelpcenter/plugins/Manpages/man1.desktop \u003e   0.60s user 0.40s system 99% cpu 1.000 total\n```\n\nSo 1s access latency is not that bad, really. This should be read-only access of the file, hence parallel access should be doable.\n\nIt might be that my OS is doing some clever caching, although the tarfile does not fit in memory (8GB). But even if it is caching that gives this performance, the same caching would also happen on the storage system.\n\nDo you consider that \"really inefficient for addressing individual files\"? Or am I missing the point?","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1555184460,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQ4Mjg3MjY5Nw==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3327#issuecomment-482872697"},"message":"You could flush filesystem cache  and try again.\nEven with 1 sec per file - 0.5 sec average, special remote works one key at a time. For 1000 files it is 8 minutes of just waiting access time scrolling through tar over and over again, which could be avoided if there was an index.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1555218029,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQ4MjkxOTE5Mg==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3327#issuecomment-482919192"},"message":"`dar` files have an index and might be an alternative. No practical experience yet.","files":null},{"type":6,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1555218029,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDE1OlVzZXJDb250ZW50RWRpdDIwOTYxMDM5Ng=="},"target":"7440486ccdbfabadc568561eec27521fd9b178810c0a9992ac3917819a7c23e1","message":"`dar` files have an index and might be an alternative. No practical experience yet. But Debian provides a static build, so deployment should be easy: https://packages.debian.org/unstable/utils/dar-static Thanks Debian!","files":null},{"type":6,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1555223839,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDE1OlVzZXJDb250ZW50RWRpdDIwOTYxOTI2NQ=="},"target":"7440486ccdbfabadc568561eec27521fd9b178810c0a9992ac3917819a7c23e1","message":"`dar` files have an index and might be an alternative. No practical experience yet. But Debian provides a static build, so deployment should be easy: https://packages.debian.org/unstable/utils/dar-static Thanks Debian!\n\nRandom access peformance is nice, individual file compression inside the archive is cool, but there is no way (that I can see) to subsequently add a file to an archive -- disqualified.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1555235109,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQ4MjkzODgwMw==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3327#issuecomment-482938803"},"message":"Pointers for further research https://stackoverflow.com/questions/429987/compression-formats-with-good-support-for-random-access-within-archives","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1555515557,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQ4NDE0NDA4NA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3327#issuecomment-484144084"},"message":"Interim conclusion: SquashFS may be, disappointingly, the most suitable solution for a (read-only) access.","files":null},{"type":4,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1555515557,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDExOkNsb3NlZEV2ZW50MjI4MzE2NTU0Nw=="},"status":2}]}